<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1942 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">The East Is Red</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="zh">东方红</bdi> / <bdi lang="zh-Latn">Dongfang Hong</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Chinese song praising Mao Zedong, made from an old folk tune, that was used in place of China's national anthem in the 1960s and 1970s.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Li Youyuan (attributed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (melody of the Shaanbei folk song &quot;Bai Ma Diao&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Chinese Communist Party and the People's Republic of China</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese (Mandarin)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Shaanbei (northern Shaanxi), China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>modern lyrics 1942; definitive version 1964</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words attributed to Li Youyuan)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a>&quot; is a revolutionary song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. Its melody came from a peasant love song of the Loess Plateau, &quot;Bai Ma Diao&quot; (White Horse Tune), also called &quot;Zhima You&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">Sesame</a> Oil), which was widely sung around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> in the 1930s and whose words often changed with the singer. The modern lyrics, attributed to Li Youyuan, a farmer from Shaanbei (northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>), were produced in 1942 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>; he is said to have been inspired by the rising sun. An earlier version may have named Liu Zhidan, a local communist hero killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> in 1936, whose name was later replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s.</p><p>The song was popular in the communist base area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> but was heard less after the party won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949, possibly because some senior leaders disagreed with its portrayal of Mao as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s saviour. Its words idealise Mao, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> its spread became one of his first efforts to present himself as a perfect hero in popular culture. In 1956 a political commissar proposed teaching it to the army; the defence minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a>, refused and called it a personality cult. His opposition to the song and to the cult around Mao contributed to his purge in 1959, after which Mao pressed the cult further.</p><p>In 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> made the song the central chorus of a stage work he created, co-produced, wrote and directed, first titled &quot;March Forward under the Banner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought&quot;. Performed by 2,000 artists with a chorus and orchestra of 1,000, it was staged repeatedly at the Great Hall of the People in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> for the 15th National Day and later filmed as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a>&quot;. The definitive version of the song was first heard in this production.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-han">Tian Han</a>, the author of the official anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a>&quot;, was purged, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a>&quot; served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s unofficial anthem; by 1966 Mao had succeeded in having it sung in place of the anthem. Loudspeakers in towns and villages played it at dawn and dusk, the Central People's Broadcasting Station opened each day with it on a set of bronze bells cast in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and broadcasts commonly began with it and closed with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-internationale">The Internationale</a>&quot;. Students sang it together at the start of the first class each morning. In 1967 the commune of Ujme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> was renamed Dongfanghong (&quot;The East Is Red&quot;) Commune. In 1969 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a> added its tune to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> Piano Concerto, and in 1970 China's first satellite took its name and sent the melody back to Earth as its first signal.</p><p>From 1 October 1978 the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a>&quot; was again played at national events, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a>&quot; lost its standing as unofficial anthem. Because of its association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> it was rarely heard after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s rise in the late 1970s, and some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> regard it as an uncomfortable reminder of the cult of personality around Mao. The tune nonetheless remains familiar: in 2009 it was voted the most popular patriotic song in a government-run internet poll, it is the chime of clocks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> railway station, the Beijing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a> Building, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> Custom House and the Drum Tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, some radio stations have used it as an interval signal, and it plays from novelty lighters bearing Mao's face sold to tourists.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> and the Communist Party, solemn and celebratory in the manner of a hymn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="7529833" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Peoples-republic-of-china-the-east-is-red-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="Q0bJmuzmi3M" data-title="The East Is Red: The Shaanbei folk-song version (CCTV)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Shaanbei folk-song version (CCTV)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel CCTV中国中央电视台. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q0bJmuzmi3M" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The East Is Red (stage production, Zhou Enlai)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the song as its central chorus; definitive version first heard here</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The East Is Red (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">film of the stage production</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Yellow River Piano Concerto (Jiang Qing, from Xian Xinghai's Yellow River Cantata)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dong Fang Hong I (China's first satellite)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named after the song; the melody was its first signal</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clock chimes of Beijing railway station, the Beijing Telegraph Building, the Shanghai Custom House and the Xi'an Drum Tower</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Interval signal of China Radio International (Indonesian service) and Xinjiang People's Radio Station</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bai Ma Diao (White Horse Tune), also called Zhima You (Sesame Oil), Shaanbei peasant love song</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Earlier version naming Liu Zhidan</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">after 1936 (possible)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier words, Mao's name later replacing Liu's</span></li><li><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1950</span><span class="sg-chain-h">closing number</span></li><li><a href="/songs/without-the-communist-party-there-would-be-no-new-china">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">included in</span></li></ul></section>
